audit.claude-opus-5.stdout.log\n?? collab/1789968388070-reviewer-debruijn-epic-audit.prompt.md\n?? collab/1789968766829-compiler-engineer-debruijn-epicfix.glm-5.3.stdout.log\n?? collab/1789968766829-compiler-engineer-debruijn-epicfix.prompt.md\n?? collab/1789975149204-compiler-engineer-debruijn-epicfix-claude.prompt.md\n?? collab/1789975149204-compiler-engineer-debruijn-epicfix-claude.ref-design-master-37dfbf54.md\n?? collab/1789975149204-compiler-engineer-debruijn-epicfix-claude.ref-epic-audit-full.md\n?? collab/1789975236632-compiler-engineer-debruijn-epicfix-claude-r2.prompt.md\n?? collab/1789975523112-reviewer-debruijn-d5.claude-sonnet-5.findings.md\n?? collab/1789975523112-reviewer-debruijn-d5.claude-sonnet-5.stdout.log\n?? collab/1789975523112-reviewer-debruijn-d5.prompt.md\n?? collab/1789975739942-compiler-engineer-debruijn-d5fix-claude.prompt.md\n?? collab/1789976750630-reviewer-debruijn-epicfix-glm.prompt.md\n?? collab/1789978107952-architect-debruijn-epicfix-review.prompt.md\n?? src/cljc/yin/vm/pipeline.cljc\n?? test/yin/vm/pipeline_test.cljc\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-bBA2kH3M' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-09-21 15:10:31.669 xcodebuild[41438:17228284]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-09-21 15:10:31.846 xcodebuild[41438:17228283] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-iaTpxVvu' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-09-21 15:10:32.371 xcodebuild[41442:17228299]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-09-21 15:10:32.548 xcodebuild[41442:17228298] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\n src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc  | 582 +++++++++++++++++++++++++----------------\n test/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc | 231 ++++++++++++++++\n 2 files changed, 594 insertions(+), 219 deletions(-)\n     1\t(ns yin.vm.pipeline\n     2\t  \"D5 of the de Bruijn projection (docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md\n     3\t   §6-§7-D5, §9): the post-emission orchestration that wires Yang's output\n     4\t   to projected persistence.\n     5\t\n     6\t   `persist-compiled!` is the composition adapter the architect's D5\n     7\t   clearance named: it emits the named :yin/* datom batch itself (or takes\n     8\t   a pre-emitted one), persists the NAMED batch first through the caller's\n     9\t   named writer, and only after named persistence succeeds materializes\n    10\t   the canonical projected envelope into a separate DaoJing content store.\n    11\t   The two sides report independently: a projection failure is a\n    12\t   :diagnostic on the projected side and never fails the named side; a\n    13\t   named failure means projected persistence is not attempted.\n    14\t\n    15\t   Ordering: only one COMPLETE root-framed batch is ever projected — never\n    16\t   a mid-emission prefix — and framing and index state are per batch, so\n    17\t   adjacent calls reusing the emitter's -16-based tempids project\n    18\t   independently. The batch passes the stream path's own framing gate,\n    19\t   `debruijn/frame-datoms`, before either side persists: a partial frame,\n    20\t   a malformed or retract datom, or anything but exactly one complete\n    21\t   frame rejects the whole call.\n    22\t\n    23\t   Dedupe is DaoJing's write idempotence alone: `dao.jing/materialize!`\n    24\t   inserts or finds :present, verifies the read-back, and never\n    25\t   overwrites; there is no pre-write lookup, and named artifacts are never\n    26\t   deduplicated by fingerprint. The result carries both identities — the\n    27\t   physical DaoJing segment address of the envelope and the semantic\n    28\t   Merkle fingerprint inside it. The physical address comes from\n    29\t   DaoJing's transitional print-based hash and is portable only between\n    30\t   implementations sharing that print rule; the Merkle fingerprint is the\n    31\t   cross-host identity.\n    32\t\n    33\t   Nothing here edits or is consumed by named consumers or runtime\n    34\t   layers: no yang.* compiler, emitter, encoder, VM, linearizer,\n    35\t   dao.stream, lease, or waitset code reads anything this namespace\n    36\t   writes.\"\n    37\t  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n    38\t            [yin.vm :as vm]\n    39\t            [yin.vm.debruijn :as debruijn]))\n    40\t\n    41\t\n    42\t(defn projected-envelope\n    43\t  \"The canonical projected persistence value: the Merkle fingerprint and\n    44\t   the projected d5 datoms as one plain-data envelope, the exact value\n    45\t   `dao.jing/materialize!` content-addresses. The fingerprint inside is\n    46\t   the semantic identity; the segment address the store returns for this\n    47\t   envelope is the physical one.\"\n    48\t  [{:keys [fingerprint records]}]\n    49\t  {:yin.debruijn/fingerprint fingerprint,\n    50\t   :yin.debruijn/datoms (debruijn/projected->datoms {:fingerprint fingerprint,\n    51\t                                                     :records records})})\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\t(defn- batch-of\n    55\t  \"The complete named batch for one request: emitted from :ast through\n    56\t   `vm/ast->datoms-with-root` — always complete, the root marker last —\n    57\t   or taken pre-emitted from :datoms, the path on which the complete-batch\n    58\t   rule is observable (an :ast cannot emit a partial batch). Exactly one\n    59\t   of the two; both or neither is a caller defect.\"\n    60\t  [request]\n    61\t  (let [ast? (some? (:ast request))\n    62\t        datoms? (some? (:datoms request))]\n    63\t    (cond\n    64\t      (and ast? datoms?)\n    65\t      (throw (ex-info \"persist-compiled! takes :ast or :datoms, not both\"\n    66\t                      {:rule :bad-request, :request-keys (vec (keys request))}))\n    67\t\n    68\t      ast? (second (vm/ast->datoms-with-root (:ast request)))\n    69\t      datoms? (vec (:datoms request))\n    70\t      :else (throw (ex-info \"persist-compiled! requires :ast or :datoms\"\n    71\t                            {:rule :bad-request, :request-keys (vec (keys request))})))))\n    72\t\n    73\t\n    74\t(defn- framing-defect\n    75\t  \"The batch's framing defect under the stream path's own gate\n    76\t   (`debruijn/frame-datoms`), or nil when it is exactly one complete\n    77\t   root-framed graph. The defect is {:rule …}: frame-datoms' own rule —\n    78\t   :partial-frame, :malformed-datom, :retract — :missing-root for a batch\n    79\t   with no frame at all, or :multiple-frames for several.\"\n    80\t  [datoms]\n    81\t  (try\n    82\t    (let [frames (debruijn/frame-datoms datoms)]\n    83\t      (case (count frames)\n    84\t        1 nil\n    85\t        0 {:rule :missing-root}\n    86\t        {:rule :multiple-frames, :frames (count frames)}))\n    87\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) t\n    88\t      (let [{:keys [status diagnostic]} (debruijn/exception-diagnostic t)]\n    89\t        (if (= :invalid-input status)\n    90\t          {:rule (:rule diagnostic)}\n    91\t          diagnostic)))))\n    92\t\n    93\t\n    94\t(defn- persist-projected!\n    95\t  \"The projected side in isolation: project the complete batch, then\n    96\t   materialize the canonical envelope. A projection diagnostic is\n    97\t   {:outcome :diagnostic, :diagnostic …}; an internal defect in the\n    98\t   projection is {:outcome :internal-error, :diagnostic …}; a store\n    99\t   failure is a :diagnostic with :rule :projected-write-failed and only\n   100\t   the store's :result and :address as :cause, never the envelope. None\n   101\t   is thrown across the boundary the named side already crossed.\"\n   102\t  [datoms projected-store]\n   103\t  (let [projection (try\n   104\t                     (debruijn/project-datoms datoms)\n   105\t                     (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) t\n   106\t                       (debruijn/exception-diagnostic t)))]\n   107\t    (if-let [status (:status projection)]\n   108\t      {:outcome (if (= :internal-error status) :internal-error :diagnostic),\n   109\t       :diagnostic (:diagnostic projection)}\n   110\t      (try\n   111\t        (let [address (jing/materialize! projected-store\n   112\t                                         (projected-envelope projection))]\n   113\t          {:outcome :ok,\n   114\t           :fingerprint (:fingerprint projection),\n   115\t           :address address})\n   116\t        (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) t\n   117\t          {:outcome :diagnostic,\n   118\t           :diagnostic {:rule :projected-write-failed,\n   119\t                        :message (ex-message t),\n   120\t                        :cause (select-keys (ex-data t) [:result :address])}})))))\n   121\t\n   122\t\n   123\t(defn persist-compiled!\n   124\t  \"Persist one compiled program's two artifacts, named first.\n   125\t\n   126\t   Takes {:ast <map AST> | :datoms <pre-emitted named batch>\n   127\t          :named-writer (fn [datoms] -> outcome map)\n   128\t          :projected-store <dao.jing content-store handle>\n   129\t          :provenance <opaque caller data>}.\n   130\t\n   131\t   :named-writer receives the complete named :yin/* batch and answers a\n   132\t   dao.stream outcome map; its :dao.stream/outcome decides the named side\n   133\t   and its whole receipt rides under :receipt. A writer that throws\n   134\t   propagates: that is a defect in the caller's own argument, exactly as\n   135\t   dao.space.transactor treats malformed input.\n   136\t\n   137\t   Returns {:named {...} :projected {...} :provenance <echo>} where the\n   138\t   two sides report independently:\n   139\t\n   140\t   | named :outcome          | when                             | projected :outcome                       |\n   141\t   |-------------------------|----------------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n   142\t   | :ok                     | writer answered :dao.stream/ok   | :ok (with :fingerprint and :address),    |\n   143\t   |                         |                                  | :diagnostic — projection or the store —, |\n   144\t   |                         |                                  | or :internal-error — a projection defect |\n   145\t   | <writer's non-ok kw>    | writer answered another keyword  | :not-attempted                           |\n   146\t   | :invalid-writer-answer  | answer carries no keyword outcome| :not-attempted                           |\n   147\t   | :rejected               | batch fails the framing gate     | :not-attempted (same :rule)              |\n   148\t\n   149\t   :provenance is opaque caller data echoed for correlation; it is never\n   150\t   interpreted and never enters either artifact.\"\n   151\t  [{:keys [named-writer projected-store provenance] :as request}]\n   152\t  (let [datoms (batch-of request)]\n   153\t    (if-let [{:keys [rule]} (framing-defect datoms)]\n   154\t      {:named {:outcome :rejected, :rule rule},\n   155\t       :projected {:outcome :not-attempted, :rule rule},\n   156\t       :provenance provenance}\n   157\t      (let [named-receipt (named-writer datoms)\n   158\t            answered (when (map? named-receipt)\n   159\t                       (get named-receipt :dao.stream/outcome))\n   160\t            named-outcome (if (keyword? answered) answered :invalid-writer-answer)]\n   161\t        (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok named-outcome)\n   162\t          {:named {:outcome named-outcome, :receipt named-receipt},\n   163\t           :projected {:outcome :not-attempted, :named-outcome named-outcome},\n   164\t           :provenance provenance}\n   165\t          {:named {:outcome :ok, :receipt named-receipt},\n   166\t           :projected (persist-projected! datoms projected-store),\n   167\t           :provenance provenance})))))\n   900\t      :bool (framed :bool (if v \"01\" \"00\"))\n   901\t      :int64 (framed :int64 (int64-le-hex v))\n   902\t      :double (framed :double (double-le-hex v))\n   903\t      :string (framed :string (normalize-nfc v))\n   904\t      :bytes (framed :bytes (apply str (map #(to-hex (bit-and % 0xff) 2) v)))\n   905\t      :keyword (framed :keyword (ident-content v))\n   906\t      :symbol (framed :symbol (ident-content v))\n   907\t      :map (let [entries (sort (map (fn [[k x]]\n   908\t                                      [(encode-value k) (encode-value x)])\n   909\t                                    v))]\n   910\t             (when (some (fn [[a b]] (= (first a) (first b)))\n   911\t                         (partition 2 1 entries))\n   912\t               (throw (ex-info \"Map keys collide in canonical encoding\"\n   913\t                               {:rule :unsupported-value, :value v})))\n   914\t             (framed :map (apply str (mapcat identity entries))))\n   915\t      :set (let [elements (sort (map encode-value v))]\n   916\t             (when (some (fn [[a b]] (= a b)) (partition 2 1 elements))\n   917\t               (throw (ex-info \"Set elements collide in canonical encoding\"\n   918\t                               {:rule :unsupported-value, :value v})))\n   919\t             (framed :set (apply str elements)))\n   920\t      :vector (framed :vector (apply str (map encode-value v)))\n   921\t      :list (framed :list (apply str (map encode-value v)))\n   922\t      (throw (ex-info \"Unsupported value in Merkle encoding\"\n   923\t                      {:rule :unsupported-value, :value v})))))\n   924\t\n   925\t\n   926\t(defn- encode-typed\n   927\t  \"Encode one descriptor slot's value under its declared type:\n   928\t   :canonical is polymorphic, the compound types frame their parts, and\n   929\t   a declared scalar type encodes by its (already validated) value\n   930\t   class.\"\n   931\t  [type v]\n   932\t  (cond\n   933\t    (= :canonical type) (encode-value v)\n   934\t    (vector? type) (if (= :tuple (first type))\n   935\t                     (framed :tuple (apply str (map encode-typed (rest type) v)))\n   936\t                     (framed :ordered-vector\n   937\t                             (str (framed :int64 (int64-le-hex (count v)))\n   938\t                                  (apply str (map #(encode-typed :ref %) v)))))\n   939\t    (or (= :ref type) (= :hash type)) (framed :ref v)\n   940\t    :else (encode-value v)))\n   941\t\n   942\t\n   943\t(def dimension-hash\n   944\t  \"The published hash domain separator (§4): the descriptor data encoded\n   945\t   through the settled canonical encoder and hashed, per datom.md's 'the\n   946\t   content hash of this subgraph IS the dimension's identity'. D0-D2\n   947\t   minted a transitional dao.jing digest; D3 re-pins it here over the\n   948\t   settled byte rules. The descriptor content is unchanged — only the\n   949\t   digest's derivation moved to the canonical encoder, so the separator\n   950\t   now separates this dimension from every other by the same bytes every\n   951\t   node hash uses.\"\n   952\t  (jing/sha256 (encode-value descriptor)))\n   953\t\n   954\t\n   955\t(defn canonical-value\n   956\t  \"A record's stored scalar is its canonical spelling (the carried D3\n   957\t   obligation): the long form for integral numbers — so 1 and 1.0 hold\n   958\t   one record content — the NFC form for strings and ident parts, and\n   959\t   the same recursively inside collections. One content address, one\n   960\t   record content: equal fingerprints imply equal :records maps.\n   961\t   Canonicalizing a map whose keys merge — {1 :a, 1.0 :b}, or two string\n   962\t   keys with one NFC form — is a key collision, diagnosed rather than\n   963\t   letting iteration order decide which entry survives; a set whose\n   964\t   elements merge — #{1 1.0}, or two spellings of é — is diagnosed the\n   965\t   same way, because the merge changes its count, not just a scalar's\n   966\t   spelling.\"\n   967\t  [v]\n   968\t  (let [class (canonical-class v)]\n   969\t    (case class\n   970\t      :nil v\n   971\t      :bool v\n   972\t      :int64 (long v)\n   973\t      :double (double v)\n   974\t      :string (normalize-nfc v)\n   975\t      :bytes v\n   976\t      :keyword (if-let [ns (namespace v)]\n   977\t                 (keyword (normalize-nfc ns) (normalize-nfc (name v)))\n   978\t                 (keyword (normalize-nfc (name v))))\n   979\t      :symbol (if-let [ns (namespace v)]\n   980\t                (symbol (normalize-nfc ns) (normalize-nfc (name v)))\n   981\t                (symbol (normalize-nfc (name v))))\n   982\t      :map (let [canonical (into {}\n   983\t                                 (map (fn [[k x]]\n   984\t                                        [(canonical-value k) (canonical-value x)]))\n   985\t                                 v)]\n   986\t             (if (< (count canonical) (count v))\n   987\t               (throw (ex-info \"Canonicalizing a map merges distinct keys\"\n   988\t                               {:rule :unsupported-value, :value v}))\n   989\t               canonical))\n   990\t      :set (let [canonical (into (empty v) (map canonical-value) v)]\n   991\t             (if (< (count canonical) (count v))\n   992\t               (throw (ex-info \"Canonicalizing a set merges distinct elements\"\n   993\t                               {:rule :unsupported-value, :value v}))\n   994\t               canonical))\n   995\t      :vector (mapv canonical-value v)\n   996\t      :list (apply list (map canonical-value v))\n   997\t      v)))\n   998\t\n   999\t\n  1000\t(defn- node-hash\n  1001\t  \"§5: hash(node) = SHA-256(dimension-hash || encode(tag-specific-slots-\n  1002\t   in-descriptor-order)). The preimage is the dimension hash's hex\n  1003\t   followed by the framed slots under the settled byte rules; the shape —\n  1004\t   descriptor order, tags, byte-count length delimiters, ordered child\n  1005\t   hashes — is what D2 fixed and D3 settled. Markers are never among the\n  1006\t   encodings: only present, non-marker slots reach here.\"\n  1007\t  [encodings]\n  1008\t  (jing/sha256 (apply str dimension-hash encodings)))\n  1009\t\n  1010\t\n  1011\t;; =============================================================================\n  1012\t;; D2: hash-consing and the projected records (§4)\n  1013\t;; =============================================================================\n  1014\t\n  1015\t(defn- slot-encodings\n  1016\t  \"The descriptor-order encodings of one record's present, non-marker\n  1017\t   slots — with the dimension hash, the only input to a node hash.\n  1018\t   Children already carry child hashes, so this is total over a record\n  1019\t   and serves both the mint and the storage reader's content check.\"\n  1020\t  [record]\n  1021\t  (for [[slot type role] dimension-slots\n  1022\t        :when (and (not= :marker role) (contains? record slot))]\n  1023\t    (encode-typed type (get record slot))))\n  1024\t\n  1025\t\n  1026\t(defn- mint-record\n  1027\t  \"Hash-cons one resolved node (§4-§5) whose children are already\n  1028\t   minted, their hashes in `child-hashes` ({slot h}, an ordered vector\n  1029\t   under :yin.debruijn/operands): the node hash over the dimension hash\n  1030\t   and the node's tag-specific slots in descriptor order, then the record\n  1031\t   — the node with child slots carrying child hashes, scalars in their\n  1032\t   canonical spellings, and :yin.debruijn/hash added. Returns [h acc']\n  1033\t   over {:hashcons preimage->h, :records h->record, :mints n}; :mints\n  1034\t   counts every call, the walk's instrumentation seam. The consing memo\n  1035\t   is keyed on the node's preimage — the concatenated slot encodings —\n  1036\t   never on Clojure =, which merges a list literal with a vector literal\n  1037\t   and the signed zeros; a preimage hit implies the identical hash and,\n  1038\t   records carrying canonical spellings, the identical record. The memo\n  1039\t   stays an optimisation only: identity is the hash either way, so\n  1040\t   traversal order never enters it.\"\n  1041\t  [node child-hashes acc]\n  1042\t  (let [acc (update acc :mints inc)\n  1043\t        record (reduce (fn [r [slot _type role]]\n  1044\t                         (cond\n  1045\t                           (or (= :marker role) (not (contains? node slot))) r\n  1046\t                           (= :child role) (assoc r slot (get child-hashes slot))\n  1047\t                           ;; the carried D3 obligation: a record's stored\n  1048\t                           ;; scalar is its canonical spelling, so equal\n  1049\t                           ;; fingerprints imply equal records\n  1050\t                           :else (assoc r slot (canonical-value (get node slot)))))\n  1051\t                       node\n  1052\t                       dimension-slots)\n  1053\t        encodings (slot-encodings record)\n  1054\t        preimage (apply str encodings)]\n  1055\t    (if-let [cached (find (:hashcons acc) preimage)]\n  1056\t      [(val cached) acc]\n  1057\t      (let [h (node-hash encodings)]\n  1058\t        [h (-> acc\n  1059\t               (assoc-in [:hashcons preimage] h)\n  1060\t               (assoc-in [:records h] (assoc record :yin.debruijn/hash h)))]))))\n  1061\t\n  1062\t\n  1063\t(defn project-datoms-counted\n  1064\t  \"`project-datoms` plus :mints, the number of record mints the walk\n  1065\t   performed — the honest instrumentation seam for the shared-subgraph\n  1066\t   bound: one mint per distinct [source-eid lexical-context] occurrence,\n  1067\t   never one per unfolded path. Output-neutral: every other key is\n  1068\t   exactly `project-datoms`'s.\"\n  1069\t  [datoms]\n  1070\t  (let [datoms (vec datoms)\n  1071\t        index (index-frame \"yin\" datoms)\n  1072\t        root-eid (frame-root datoms)\n  1073\t        [root fingerprint {:keys [records mints]}]\n  1074\t        (project-node index\n  1075\t                      root-eid\n  1076\t                      []\n  1077\t                      {:memo {}, :hashcons {}, :records {}, :mints 0}\n  1078\t                      #{}\n  1079\t                      [])]\n  1080\t    {:root root, :fingerprint fingerprint, :records records, :mints mints}))\n  1081\t\n  1082\t\n  1083\t(defn project-datoms\n  1084\t  \"§6's pure projection of one complete rooted graph of :yin/* AST\n  1085\t   datoms, through §2 validation and §3 scope resolution to D2's Merkle\n  1086\t   records. Returns {:root node, :fingerprint h, :records {h record}}:\n  1087\t   the resolved semantic graph under :root (whose wrapper key is the\n  1088\t   graph's root marker — the node map carries none, so it equals the\n  1089\t   same term appearing as a subterm), the root node hash as\n  1090\t   :fingerprint, and the hash-consed projected records — each the\n  1091\t   resolved node with child slots carrying child hashes and\n  1092\t   :yin.debruijn/hash h added. A bound occurrence carries\n  1093\t   :yin.debruijn/bound [frame-depth position], a free one\n  1094\t   :yin.debruijn/free name, and a lambda's binder is its\n  1095\t   :yin.debruijn/arity.\n  1096\t\n  1097\t   Input order, source tempids, t, m, and non-:yin/* namespaces never\n  1098\t   enter the result, at graph level or hash level. Every defect is\n  1099\t   ex-info carrying :rule (and, where the walk has descended, :entity\n  1100\t   and :path). For a stream carrying several adjacent graphs, frame it\n  1101\t   first with `frame-datoms` and project each frame — the per-frame fact\n  1102\t   index resets there.\"\n  1103\t  [datoms]\n  1104\t  (dissoc (project-datoms-counted datoms) :mints))\n  1105\t\n  1106\t\n  1107\t;; =============================================================================\n  1108\t;; D2: the d5 storage adapter (§4)\n  1109\t;; =============================================================================\n  1110\t\n  1111\t(defn projected->datoms\n  1112\t  \"The d5 storage adapter's write half (§4): one projection's records as\n  1113\t   d5 datoms [e a v t m]. Entities are local handles minted from\n  1114\t   dao.datom's first-user-id in a deterministic root-down, descriptor\n  1115\t   slot order walk — handles, ordinals, and row count are storage layout\n  1116\t   only, never identity. Each entity carries :yin.debruijn/hash h; child\n  1117\t   slots carry child hashes; :yin.debruijn/operands is one ordered\n  1118\t   vector datom, never cardinality-many; and the root entity is\n  1119\t   explicitly marked :yin.debruijn/root true — the root-hash marker, its\n  1120\t   :yin.debruijn/hash being the fingerprint. t 0 and the assert op are\n  1121\t   the adapter's fixed provenance, mirroring the named emitter's\n  1122\t   defaults.\"\n  1123\t  [{:keys [fingerprint records]}]\n  1124\t  (let [t 0\n  1125\t        m datom/default-op\n  1126\t        out (atom [])\n  1127\t        emitted (atom #{})\n  1128\t        next-eid (atom (dec datom/first-user-id))\n  1129\t        emit! (fn [e a v] (swap! out conj [e a v t m]))\n  1130\t        walk (fn walk\n  1131\t               [h]\n  1132\t               ;; one entity per hash: a record reachable by several paths\n  1133\t               ;; is emitted once, and the slot datoms already pointing at\n  1134\t               ;; its hash are the sharing\n  1135\t               (when-not (contains? @emitted h)\n  1136\t                 (let [e (swap! next-eid inc)\n  1137\t                       ;; the records came from the projection itself, so a\n  1138\t                       ;; missing one is a projection defect, never input:\n  1139\t                       ;; its rule is one `exception-diagnostic` classifies\n  1140\t                       ;; :internal-error\n  1141\t                       record (or (get records h)\n  1142\t                                  (throw (ex-info\n  1143\t                                           \"Projected record set does not contain a child hash\"\n  1144\t                                           {:rule :missing-record, :hash h})))]\n  1145\t                   (swap! emitted conj h)\n  1146\t                   (emit! e :yin.debruijn/hash h)\n  1147\t                   (doseq [[slot type role] dimension-slots\n  1148\t                           :when (and (not= :marker role) (contains? record slot))]\n  1149\t                     (let [v (get record slot)]\n  1150\t                       (emit! e slot v)\n  1151\t                       (when (= :child role)\n  1152\t                         (if (and (vector? type) (= :ordered-vector (first type)))\n  1153\t                           (doseq [child v] (walk child))\n  1154\t                           (walk v)))))\n  1155\t                   (when (= h fingerprint)\n  1156\t                     (emit! e :yin.debruijn/root true)))))]\n  1157\t    (walk fingerprint)\n  1158\t    @out))\n  1159\t\n  1160\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"defn-? (check-record|project-node|frame-datoms|exception-diagnostic)|check-record|memo|missing-slot|canonical|macro\\\\?|multiple-frames|missing-root|budget\\\" src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc && nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc | sed -n '300,760p' && nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc | sed -n '760,910p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
